  "description": "One-minute skill: Cognitive Reserve.",
  "is_free": false,
  "published": true,
  "story": {
    "hook": {
      "text": "Two patients have identical Alzheimer's pathology on autopsy. The other had severe dementia for years.",
      "visual": "🔬"
    },
    "buildup": {
      "text": "Cognitive reserve is the brain's ability to find alternate routes to complete tasks when primary pathways fail.",
      "visual": "🛤️"
    },
    "discovery": {
      "text": "Education, occupational complexity, social engagement, and bilingualism all build cognitive reserve by creating denser, more.",
      "visual": "🌐"
    },
    "twist": {
      "text": "Cognitive reserve does not prevent brain disease. The brain compensates so well that symptoms stay hidden until damage is severe.",
      "visual": "🎭"
    },
    "climax": {
      "text": "Building cognitive reserve throughout adulthood may delay dementia onset by years, buying time even when pathology is progressing.",
      "visual": "⏳"
    },
    "punchline": {
      "text": "A well-connected brain can lose neurons and still function.",
      "visual": "🛡️"
    }
  },
  "quiz": {
    "question": "What is cognitive reserve?",
    "options": [
      "The brain's ability to compensate for damage using alternate pathways",
      "The total number of neurons at birth",
      "The maximum memory storage capacity of the brain"
    ],
    "correct": 0
  },
